.claude/specs/SPEC-005.json:3:  "purpose": "Define the complete, compilable, verbatim shape and observable serde contract of monokl's dependency/export/JSX/language-data type cluster -- DependencyRecord, DependencyBinding, BindingKind, DependencyTarget, RustPathAnchor, ExportRecord, JsxAttribute, JsxElementEntry, LangData (plus its two methods), and the five per-language data structs (TsData, RustData, PythonData, GoData, JavaData) -- per docs/spec/01-core-architecture.md Section 4 (lines 312-452), so a developer implementing types.rs, or any downstream analyzer constructing these values, never has to guess a variant, field, serde tag strategy, or rename. This cluster is where two structurally different serde enum-tagging strategies coexist (DependencyTarget's internally-tagged `tag = \"kind\"`, which flattens each variant's fields into the same JSON object as the tag, versus LangData's adjacently-tagged `tag = \"language\", content = \"data\"`, which keeps the tag and the variant's payload in two separate keys) and where some #[serde(rename = ...)] overrides are functionally necessary to produce the intended JSON string (RustPathAnchor::Selff -> \"self\"; LangData::Ts -> \"typescript\") while others are redundant with what the enclosing rename_all = \"camelCase\" would already produce on its own -- precision an implementer would otherwise have to reverse-engineer from serde's own transform rules rather than read directly.",
.claude/specs/SPEC-005.json:4:  "scope": "DependencyRecord (3 fields), DependencyBinding (3 fields), BindingKind (5 variants), DependencyTarget (an internally-tagged, #[non_exhaustive] 3-variant enum), RustPathAnchor (a #[non_exhaustive] 4-variant enum with one functionally necessary rename), ExportRecord (3 fields), JsxAttribute (4 fields), JsxElementEntry (4 fields), LangData (an adjacently-tagged, #[non_exhaustive] 5-variant enum plus its ts()/jsx_elements() methods), TsData (3 fields, the only one of the five per-language data structs with real content), and RustData/PythonData/GoData/JavaData (four structurally identical empty placeholder structs) -- all verbatim per 01-core-architecture.md:312-452, including the \"Post-research correction\" note at lines 397-400 explaining why Go/Java replace an earlier CSharp variant. docs/spec/03-multi-language-platform.md independently restates RustPathAnchor (03-multi-language-platform.md:2405-2417), DependencyTarget (2419-2442), and LangData plus its ts()/jsx_elements() methods (2381-2403) in its own 'types added' changelog block, each confirmed semantically identical to the canonical source -- differing only in single-line-vs-multi-line attribute formatting, no drift found. It locks each type's exact field/variant order, derive list, and serde attributes; the two distinct enum-tagging JSON shapes these types produce; which #[serde(rename = ...)] overrides are necessary versus redundant; and the observable deserialize consequences of #[non_exhaustive] (a Rust-level match-exhaustiveness attribute with no effect on serde's generated (de)serialize code) versus the presence or absence of #[serde(default)] on Vec-typed fields, which -- unlike Option-typed fields -- do not get implicit missing-key leniency from serde's derive macro without that attribute.",
.claude/specs/SPEC-005.json:8:    "TsData/RustData/PythonData/GoData/JavaData's population semantics -- which analyzer sets which field, and when -- owned by each language analyzer's own implementation track.",
.claude/specs/SPEC-005.json:9:    "Whether GoData/JavaData (or PythonData) gain real fields once their analyzers land -- that is those future analyzer tracks' job; this track locks only their current, empty shape and the documented reason they're empty today.",
.claude/specs/SPEC-005.json:102:      "criterion": "LangData (01-core-architecture.md:402-416) is an adjacently tagged enum: #[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)] (no Copy/PartialEq/Eq), #[serde(rename_all = \"camelCase\", tag = \"language\", content = \"data\")], #[non_exhaustive], with exactly 5 variants in this order: Ts(TsData), Rust(RustData), Python(PythonData), Go(GoData), Java(JavaData). Adjacent tagging keeps the tag and the variant's payload in two separate keys of the same object -- e.g. the Ts variant serializes as {\"language\":\"typescript\",\"data\":{...TsData's own fields...}} -- a structurally different JSON shape from DependencyTarget's internal tagging (AC-005), which flattens variant fields directly alongside the tag key with no separate content wrapper. Each variant carries its own explicit #[serde(rename = ...)] tag value: Ts->\"typescript\", Rust->\"rust\", Python->\"python\", Go->\"go\", Java->\"java\".",
.claude/specs/SPEC-005.json:120:      "criterion": "The Post-research correction note at 01-core-architecture.md:397-400 states Go and Java replace an earlier CSharp variant on LangData, reflecting a language-roadmap decision to drop C# in favor of Go and Java (docs/spec/05-research-and-decisions.md \u00a76), and that GoData and JavaData are empty placeholders today, identical in shape to PythonData -- populated once those analyzers land. This is forward-looking, documented scope, not an oversight: LangData's own #[non_exhaustive] attribute (AC-012) means adding further LangData variants for additional languages does not require this spec to be revised for external-crate exhaustive-match compatibility on LangData itself. That protection is enum-level only and does not extend to GoData/JavaData's own fields -- none of the four per-language data structs (AC-018) carries #[non_exhaustive] on its own struct declaration, so adding real fields to GoData or JavaData later is a separate compatibility question this track does not resolve, not one LangData's attribute already covers.",
.claude/specs/SPEC-005.json:138:      "criterion": "RustData, PythonData, GoData, and JavaData (01-core-architecture.md:438-452) are four structurally identical, field-less structs: pub struct XData {} for each, all sharing #[derive(Debug, Clone, Default, Serialize, Deserialize)] and #[serde(rename_all = \"camelCase\")] (the rename_all attribute is vacuous for a field-less struct -- present for consistency with the cluster's other types, not because it changes any observable behavior). Each serializes as an empty JSON object ({}) and deserializes successfully from {}; each XData::default() (via the derived Default impl) is simply XData {}.",
